# ==============================================================================
# Limits Module - Centralized System Constraints
# ==============================================================================
#
# This module centralizes all magic numbers and system limits used throughout
# the email parser. It provides a single source of truth for:
# - Size limits (email size, attachment size, filename size)
# - Count limits (number of attachments, headers, message IDs)
# - Structural limits (multipart depth, boundary length)
# - Iteration limits (to prevent DoS attacks)
#
# ## Purpose
#
# Centralizing limits makes it easy to:
# - Find all system constraints in one place
# - Change limits consistently across the codebase
# - Understand system boundaries and security constraints
# - Audit all limits for compliance or performance reasons
#
# ## Architecture
#
# All constants are public and organized by category:
# 1. **Size Limits**: Maximum sizes for emails, attachments, filenames
# 2. **Count Limits**: Maximum counts for attachments, headers, message IDs
# 3. **Structural Limits**: Maximum depth, boundary length, etc.
# 4. **Iteration Limits**: Maximum iterations for parsing loops
#
# ## Design Decisions
#
# ### Centralized over Distributed
#
# All limits are defined here rather than scattered across multiple files:
# - Single source of truth
# - Easier to maintain and audit
# - Consistent enforcement across the codebase
# - Self-documenting system constraints
#
# ### Documentation with Rationales
#
# Each constant includes comments explaining:
# - Why the limit exists
# - RFC references (if applicable)
# - Real-world usage patterns
# - Security implications (DoS prevention)
# - Performance considerations
#
# ## Module Interactions
#
# This module is imported by:
# - **emailparser**: Uses all limits for parsing configuration
# - **emailparser.validation**: Uses MAX_BOUNDARY_LENGTH
# - **emailparser.message_id**: Uses MAX_DOMAIN_SIZE
# - **emailparser.header**: Uses MAX_FILENAME_SIZE
#
# This module has no dependencies on other emailparser modules.
#
# ## Public API
#
# All constants are public and intended for use throughout the emailparser
# package. Functions are not provided as these are compile-time constants.
#
# ## RFC Compliance
#
# Many limits are based on RFC specifications:
# - RFC 5322: Email message format
# - RFC 2045/2046: MIME specifications
# - RFC 1035: DNS domain name limits

# ========== Size Limits ==========

# Maximum email size in bytes (50 MB)
#
# Rationale:
# - Gmail limits: 25 MB for attachments, ~50 MB total message size
# - DoS prevention: prevents memory exhaustion from oversized emails
# - Base64 expansion: 33% overhead for attachments means a 50 MB limit
#   accommodates ~37.5 MB of actual content after encoding
# - Performance: larger emails significantly increase processing time
# - Most legitimate emails are < 10 MB
#
# RFC reference: RFC 5322 does not specify a maximum size
let pub MAX_EMAIL_SIZE = 50_000_000

# Maximum attachment size in bytes (25 MB)
#
# Rationale:
# - Gmail attachment limit: 25 MB
# - Outlook/O365 attachment limit: 20-150 MB depending on plan
# - DoS prevention: prevents memory exhaustion from large attachments
# - Storage efficiency: limits disk usage for parsed emails
# - Base64 overhead: accounts for 33% encoding expansion
#
# RFC reference: RFC 2045 section 6.8 (Content-Transfer-Encoding)
let pub MAX_ATTACHMENT_SIZE = 25_000_000

# Maximum filename length (255 chars)
#
# Rationale:
# - Common filesystem limit (ext4, NTFS, APFS)
# - Historical DOS filename limit (8.3 format)
# - Email standard practice for Content-Disposition filename parameter
# - Prevents filesystem errors when saving attachments
#
# RFC reference: RFC 2183 (Content-Disposition)
let pub MAX_FILENAME_SIZE = 255

# Maximum domain name length (255 chars)
#
# Rationale:
# - DNS specification limit (RFC 1035)
# - FQDN (fully qualified domain name) maximum length
# - Ensures compatibility with all DNS resolvers
# - Prevents buffer overflows in DNS queries
#
# RFC reference: RFC 1035 section 2.3.4
let pub MAX_DOMAIN_SIZE = 255

# ========== Count Limits ==========

# Maximum number of attachments to parse (100)
#
# Rationale:
# - Storage limits: prevents excessive memory/disk usage
# - Real-world usage: most emails have < 10 attachments
# - User experience: large attachment lists are rarely useful
# - DoS prevention: prevents abuse via many small attachments
# - Processing time: each attachment requires decoding and validation
#
# RFC reference: RFC 2045 section 6 (Content-Transfer-Encoding)
let pub MAX_ATTACHMENTS = 100

# Maximum number of headers to parse (1000)
#
# Rationale:
# - DoS prevention: prevents header parsing DoS attacks
# - Real-world usage: most emails have < 50 headers
# - Memory efficiency: each header is stored in memory
# - Performance: header parsing is O(n) where n is number of headers
# - RFC compliance: RFC 5322 doesn't specify a limit, but typical limits exist
#
# RFC reference: RFC 5322 section 2.2 (Header Fields)
let pub MAX_HEADERS = 1000

# Maximum header value length in bytes (8192)
#
# Rationale:
# - DoS prevention: prevents memory exhaustion from folded headers
# - RFC 5322: allows line folding but doesn't specify limit
# - Postfix: default limit is 102400 but many use smaller values
# - Sendmail: header size limit of 32768 bytes total
# - Gmail: practical limit around 10KB per header
# - Performance: prevents O(n) string concatenation attacks
# - Folding protection: prevents millions of continuation lines
#
# This limit applies to the unfolded header value (after folding processing).
# Individual lines may be shorter, but the combined value must fit within
# this limit. In strict mode, exceeding this limit returns an error.
# In permissive mode, the header value is truncated.
#
# RFC reference: RFC 5322 section 2.2.3 (Long Header Fields)
let pub MAX_HEADER_VALUE_LENGTH = 8192

# Maximum number of message IDs to parse (100)
#
# Rationale:
# - Threading limits: prevents excessive thread ancestry processing
# - Real-world usage: typical email threads have < 20 references
# - Gmail/Outlook: both cap displayed thread history at reasonable depths
# - DoS prevention: prevents abuse via massive References headers
# - Performance: each message ID is stored and processed
#
# RFC reference: RFC 5322 section 3.6.4 (Message-ID Header Field)
let pub MAX_MESSAGE_IDS = 100

# ========== Structural Limits ==========

# Maximum nesting depth for multipart messages (10 levels)
#
# Rationale:
# - Stack overflow prevention: limits recursive parsing depth
# - Real-world usage: most emails have 2-4 nesting levels max
#   - Level 1: multipart/mixed (message root)
#   - Level 2: multipart/alternative (text + HTML)
#   - Level 3: multipart/related (HTML + inline images)
#   - Level 4+: rarely used
# - DoS prevention: prevents pathological nested multipart attacks
# - Processing time: O(depth) complexity for nested structures
#
# RFC reference: RFC 2046 section 5.1.3 (Nested Multipart)
let pub MAX_MULTIPART_DEPTH = 10

# Maximum boundary string length (70 characters)
#
# Rationale:
# - RFC 2046 specification: boundary = 0*69<bchars> bcharspace
# - Maximum length is 70 characters (69 bchars + optional space)
# - Ensures compatibility with all MIME parsers
# - Prevents header overflow attacks
#
# RFC reference: RFC 2046 section 5.1.1 (Multipart Media Type)
let pub MAX_BOUNDARY_LENGTH = 70

# ========== Iteration Limits ==========

# Maximum iterations for header decoding (1000)
#
# Rationale:
# - RFC 2047 encoded-word parsing: limits number of encoded words processed
# - DoS prevention: prevents infinite loops from pathological inputs
# - Real-world usage: most headers have < 50 encoded words
# - Performance: each iteration performs string operations and decoding
# - Safety: ensures bounded execution time for header parsing
#
# RFC reference: RFC 2047 section 6 (Encoded-Word Syntax)
let pub MAX_HEADER_ITERATIONS = 1000
